helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3150536				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_CHECKBOX_TP_DROPCAPS_CB_SWITCH\"\>Applies the drop cap settings to the selected paragraph.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155626				0	en-ZA	Whole word				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3154554				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_CHECKBOX_TP_DROPCAPS_CB_WORD\"\>Displays the first letter of the first word in the paragraph as a drop cap, and the remaining letters of the word as large type.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154505				0	en-ZA	Number of characters				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3149881	8			0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_NUMERICFIELD_TP_DROPCAPS_FLD_DROPCAPS\"\>Enter the number of characters to convert to drop caps. \</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150932				0	en-ZA	Lines				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3148391				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_NUMERICFIELD_TP_DROPCAPS_FLD_LINES\"\>Enter the number of lines that you want the drop cap to extend downward from the first line of the paragraph. Shorter paragraphs will not get drop caps.\</ahelp\> The selection is limited to 2-9 lines.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149030				0	en-ZA	Distance from text				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3153926				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_METRICFIELD_TP_DROPCAPS_FLD_DISTANCE\"\>Enter the amount of space to leave between the drop caps and the rest of the text in the paragraph.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153723				0	en-ZA	Contents				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154638				0	en-ZA	Text				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3147569				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_EDIT_TP_DROPCAPS_EDT_TEXT\"\>Enter the text that you want to display as drop caps instead of the first letters of the paragraph.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150763				0	en-ZA	Character Style				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\05030400.xhp	0	help	par_id3151181				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"SW_LISTBOX_TP_DROPCAPS_BOX_TEMPLATE\"\>Select the formatting style that you want to apply to the drop caps.\</ahelp\> To use the formatting style of the current paragraph, select [None].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\selection_mode.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-ZA	Selection Mode				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\selection_mode.xhp	0	help	hd_id4177678				0	en-ZA	\<link href=\"text/swriter/01/selection_mode.xhp\"\>Selection Mode\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\selection_mode.xhp	0	help	par_id2962126				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>Choose the selection mode from the submenu: normal selection mode, or block selection mode.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\selection_mode.xhp	0	help	par_id9816278				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>In normal selection mode, you can select multi-line text including the line ends.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\selection_mode.xhp	0	help	par_id3097323				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>In block selection mode, you can select a rectangular block of text.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-ZA	Defining Conditions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145828				0	en-ZA	\<bookmark_value\>logical expressions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>formulating conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>conditions; in fields and sections\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>fields;defining conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>sections;defining conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>variables; in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>user data;in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>databases;in conditions\</bookmark_value\>      \<bookmark_value\>hiding; database fields\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145828				0	en-ZA	\<variable id=\"defining_conditions\"\>\<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04090200.xhp\"\>Defining Conditions\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145242				0	en-ZA	Conditions are logical expressions that you can use to control the display of \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04090000.xhp\"\>fields\</link\> and \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/04020000.xhp\"\>sections\</link\> in your document. Although the following examples apply to fields, they also apply to sections.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147171				0	en-ZA	You can define conditions for the following field types:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151185				0	en-ZA	Conditional text: displays text A if the condition is true, or text B if the condition is false.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149289				0	en-ZA	Hidden text: hides the contents of the field if the condition is true.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145412				0	en-ZA	Hidden paragraph: hides the paragraph if the condition is true.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147515				0	en-ZA	Any record and next record: controls the access to database records.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149802	18			0	en-ZA	The simplest way to define a condition is to type the logical expression directly in a \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box using the following values:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153677				0	en-ZA	TRUE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152960				0	en-ZA	The condition is always met. You can also enter any value not equal to 0 as the conditional text.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155900				0	en-ZA	FALSE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154191				0	en-ZA	The condition is not met. You can also enter the value 0.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147090	23			0	en-ZA	If you leave the \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box empty, the condition is interpreted as not being met.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148980				0	en-ZA	When you define a condition, use the same \<link href=\"text/swriter/02/14020000.xhp\"\>elements\</link\> for defining a formula, namely comparative operators, mathematical and statistical functions, number formats, variables and constants.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153638				0	en-ZA	You can use the following types of variables when you define a condition:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155135				0	en-ZA	Predefined \<link href=\"text/swriter/02/14020000.xhp\"\>$[officename] variables\</link\> that use statistics on document properties				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156273				0	en-ZA	Custom variables, that are a created with the "Set variable" field				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149174				0	en-ZA	Variables based on user data				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145781				0	en-ZA	Variables based on the contents of database fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155916				0	en-ZA	You cannot use internal variables, such as page and chapter numbers, in condition expression.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151375				0	en-ZA	Conditions and Variables				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150122				0	en-ZA	The following examples use a variable called "x":				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147417				0	en-ZA	x == 1 or x EQ 1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147500				0	en-ZA	The condition is true if "x" is equal to 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145259				0	en-ZA	x != 1 or x NEQ 1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150551				0	en-ZA	The condition is true if "x" does not equal 1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147749				0	en-ZA	sinx == 0				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146345				0	en-ZA	The condition is true if "x" is a multiple of pi.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149846				0	en-ZA	To use comparative operators with strings, the operands must be bounded by double quotation marks:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151078				0	en-ZA	x == "ABC" or x EQ "ABC"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150162				0	en-ZA	Checks if variable "x" contains (true) the "ABC" string, or not (false).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153301				0	en-ZA	x == "" or x EQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156120				0	en-ZA	or				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156133				0	en-ZA	!x or NOT x				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156112				0	en-ZA	Checks if the variable "x" contains an empty string.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150097				0	en-ZA	The "equal" comparative operator must be represented by two equal signs (==) in a condition. For example, if you define a variable "x" with the value of 1, you can enter the condition as x==1.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148791				0	en-ZA	User Data				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150028				0	en-ZA	You can include user data when you define conditions. To change your user data, choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - $[officename] - User data\</emph\>. User data must be entered in the form of strings. You can query the user data with "==" (EQ), "!=" (NEQ), or "!"(NOT).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153124				0	en-ZA	The following table lists user data variables and their meanings:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149632				0	en-ZA	Variable				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150662				0	en-ZA	Meaning				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154026				0	en-ZA	user_firstname				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149953				0	en-ZA	First name				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147272				0	en-ZA	user_lastname				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149601				0	en-ZA	Last name				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150770				0	en-ZA	user_initials				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155529				0	en-ZA	Initials				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148705				0	en-ZA	user_company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148728				0	en-ZA	Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155361				0	en-ZA	user_street				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154222				0	en-ZA	Street				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145108				0	en-ZA	user_country				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146885				0	en-ZA	Country				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149580				0	en-ZA	user_zipcode				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156241				0	en-ZA	Postal code				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148922				0	en-ZA	user_city				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148945				0	en-ZA	City				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156053				0	en-ZA	user_title				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159219				0	en-ZA	Title				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156435				0	en-ZA	user_position				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145178				0	en-ZA	Position				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150797				0	en-ZA	user_tel_work				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150820				0	en-ZA	Business telephone number				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150894				0	en-ZA	user_tel_home				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155320				0	en-ZA	Home telephone number				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154400				0	en-ZA	user_fax				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153363				0	en-ZA	Fax number				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153390				0	en-ZA	user_email				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154948				0	en-ZA	E-mail address				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145603				0	en-ZA	user_state				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150129				0	en-ZA	State (not in all $[officename] versions)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150147				0	en-ZA	For example, to hide a paragraph, text, or a section from a user with a specific initial, such as "LM", enter the condition: user_initials=="LM".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154115				0	en-ZA	Conditions and Database Fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154128				0	en-ZA	You can define conditions for accessing databases, or database fields. For example, you can check the contents of a database field from a condition, or use database fields in logical expressions. The following table lists a few more examples of using databases in conditions:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156066				0	en-ZA	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156088				0	en-ZA	Meaning				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155948				0	en-ZA	Database.Table.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148673				0	en-ZA	Database.Table.Company NEQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148687				0	en-ZA	Database.Table.Company != ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155373				0	en-ZA	The condition is true if the COMPANY field is not empty. (In the first example, no operator is required.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149977				0	en-ZA	!Database.Table.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149991				0	en-ZA	NOT Database.Table.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150004				0	en-ZA	Database.Table.Company EQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3146911				0	en-ZA	Database.Table.Company ==""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148733				0	en-ZA	Returns TRUE if the COMPANY field is empty.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148762				0	en-ZA	Database.Table.Company !="Sun"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153016				0	en-ZA	Database.Table.Company NEQ "Sun"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153040				0	en-ZA	Returns TRUE if the current entry in the COMPANY field is not "Sun". (Exclamation sign represents a logical NOT.)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154605				0	en-ZA	Database.Table.Firstname AND Database.Table.Name				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153059				0	en-ZA	Returns TRUE if the record contains the first and the last name.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3159247				0	en-ZA	Note the difference between the boolean NOT "!" and the comparative operator not equal "!=" (NEQ).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153876				0	en-ZA	When you refer to a database field in a condition, use the form Databasename.Tablename.Fieldname. If one of the names contains a character that is an operator, such as a minus sign (-), enclose the name in square brackets, for example, Databasename.[Table-name].Fieldname. Never use spaces inside field names.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153891				0	en-ZA	Example: Hiding an Empty Database Field				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150051				0	en-ZA	You may want to create a condition that hides an empty field, for example, if the COMPANY field is empty for some of the data records.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150067				0	en-ZA	Select the \<emph\>Hidden Paragraph\</emph\> list entry, and type the following condition: Addressbook.Addresses.Company EQ ""				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147110				0	en-ZA	or type the following				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147123				0	en-ZA	NOT Addressbook.Addresses.Company				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147136				0	en-ZA	If the COMPANY database field is empty, the condition is true and the paragraph is hidden.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150232				0	en-ZA	To display hidden paragraphs on the screen, you can choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Writer - Formatting Aids\</emph\>, and clear the \<emph\>Fields: Hidden paragraphs\</emph\> check box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145218				0	en-ZA	Examples of Conditions in Fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3145231				0	en-ZA	The following examples use the Conditional text field, although they can be applied to any fields that can be linked to a condition. The syntax used for conditions is also used for the Hidden text, Hidden paragraph, Any record or Next record fields.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150311				0	en-ZA	To display conditional text based on the number of pages:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150333				0	en-ZA	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Fields - Other\</emph\>, and then click the \<emph\>Functions\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147471				0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Type\</emph\> list, click "Conditional text".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154294	63			0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box, type "page == 1".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154319				0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Then\</emph\> box, type "There is only one page".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150640	65			0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Or \</emph\>box, type "There are several pages".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153086				0	en-ZA	Click \<emph\>Insert\</emph\>, and then click \<emph\>Close\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155814				0	en-ZA	To display conditional text based on a user-defined Variable				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155836				0	en-ZA	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Fields - Other\</emph\>, and then click the \<emph\>Variables\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155109	68			0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Type \</emph\>list, click "Set Variable".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147008	139			0	en-ZA	In the\<emph\> Name\</emph\> box, type "Profit".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147032	140			0	en-ZA	In the\<emph\> Value\</emph\> box, type "5000".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152974				0	en-ZA	Click \<emph\>Insert\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3152998				0	en-ZA	Click the \<emph\>Functions\</emph\> tab, and click "Conditional text" in the \<emph\>Type\</emph\> list.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150952				0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Condition\</emph\> box, type "Profit < 5000".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156291				0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Then\</emph\> box, type "Target is not met".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3156317	72			0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Or \</emph\>box, type "Target is met".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154366				0	en-ZA	Click \<emph\>Insert\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154389				0	en-ZA	To edit the contents of the "Profit" variable, double-click the variable field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155573				0	en-ZA	To display conditional text based on the contents of a database field:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155587				0	en-ZA	The first part of this example inserts a space between the "First Name" and "Last Name" fields in a document, and the second part inserts text based on the contents of a field. This example requires that an address data source is registered with $[officename].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150523				0	en-ZA	Choose \<emph\>Insert - Fields - Other\</emph\>, and then click the \<emph\>Database\</emph\> tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148811	75			0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Type \</emph\>list, click "Mail merge fields".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3148841	76			0	en-ZA	In the\<emph\> Database selection\</emph\> box, double-click an address book, click "First Name", and then click\<emph\> Insert\</emph\>. Repeat for "Last Name".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3147549	78			0	en-ZA	In the document, place the cursor between the two fields, press Space, and then return to the \<emph\>Fields \</emph\>dialogue:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150416				0	en-ZA	Click the \<emph\>Functions\</emph\> tab, and then click "Conditional text" in the \<emph\>Type\</emph\> list.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153589	146			0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box, type: "Addressbook.addresses.firstname".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153615	80			0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Then \</emph\>box, type a space and leave the \<emph\>Or \</emph\>box blank.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3153562				0	en-ZA	You can now use a condition to insert text based on the contents of the First Name field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150574	147			0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Fields \</emph\>dialogue, click the \<emph\>Functions \</emph\>tab.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150605	85			0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Type \</emph\>box, click "Conditional text".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151277	86			0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Condition \</emph\>box, type: Addressbook.addresses.firstname == "Michael"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3151303	87			0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Then \</emph\>box, type "Dear".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149138				0	en-ZA	In the \<emph\>Else\</emph\> box, type "Hello".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\04090200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149163				0	en-ZA	Click \<emph\>Insert\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06110000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-ZA	Calculate				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06110000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154505				0	en-ZA	\<link href=\"text/swriter/01/06110000.xhp\" name=\"Calculate\"\>Calculate\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06110000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150021				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".uno:CalculateSel\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Calculates the selected formula and copies the result to the clipboard.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06190000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-ZA	Update All				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06190000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145824				0	en-ZA	\<link href=\"text/swriter/01/06190000.xhp\" name=\"Update All\"\>Update All\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\06190000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153004				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".uno:UpdateAll\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Updates all links, fields, indexes, tables of contents, and page formatting in the current document.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-ZA	Navigation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147745				0	en-ZA	Navigation				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149844				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SCRL_NAVI\"\>If you click this icon in the Navigator or in the lower right of the document window, a toolbar will appear which enables you to choose among the existing targets within a document.\</ahelp\> You can then use the up and down arrow icons to position the text cursor in the document on the previous or next target.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153293				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SCRL_PAGEUP\"\>Click the up button to scroll to the previous page or object.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156098				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_SCRL_PAGEDOWN\"\>Click the down button to scroll to the next page or object.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155076				0	en-ZA	By default, as long as you have not selected any other entry, the arrow buttons jump to the previous or the next page in the document. The arrow buttons are black if you are browsing through pages and blue if you jump to other objects.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154330				0	en-ZA	The entries largely correspond to those in the \<link href=\"text/swriter/01/02110000.xhp\" name=\"Navigator\"\>Navigator\</link\> selection box. You can also select other jump destinations. An example are the reminders, which you can set with the \<emph\>Set Reminder\</emph\> icon in the Navigator. You can select an object from among the following options on the \<emph\>Navigation\</emph\> toolbar: table, text frame, graphics, OLE object, page, headings, reminder, drawing object, control field, section, bookmark, selection, footnote, note, index entry, table formula, wrong table formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148783				0	en-ZA	For table formulae, you can either jump to all table formulae located within your document or only to the incorrect ones. For incorrect formulae, you jump only to formulae that have resulted in errors. The program skips over formulae with resulting errors (those that reference incorrect formulae).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150031				0	en-ZA	Working With the Navigation Toolbar				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150045				0	en-ZA	Open the \<emph\>Navigation\</emph\> toolbar by clicking on its icon located in the vertical scrollbar. You can break the toolbar away from its place by dragging and arrange it on the screen.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153141				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_NAVI_VS\"\>Click the icon for the type of objects you want to browse through. Then click one of the "Previous Object" or "Next Object" arrow buttons. The names of these buttons indicate the type of object you have selected. The text cursor is placed on whichever object you have selected.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149968				0	en-ZA	You can configure $[officename] according to your specific preferences for navigating within a document. To do this, choose \<link href=\"text/shared/01/06140000.xhp\" name=\"Tools - Customize\"\>\<emph\>Tools - Customize\</emph\>\</link\>. The various tables for adapting \<link href=\"text/shared/01/06140100.xhp\" name=\"menus\"\>menus\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/shared/01/06140200.xhp\" name=\"keyboard input\"\>keyboard input\</link\> or toolbars contain various functions for navigation within the document under the "Navigate" area. In this way you can jump to the index tags in the document with the "To Next/Previous Index Tag" functions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155338				0	en-ZA	\<bookmark_value\>searching; repeating a search\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155338				0	en-ZA	Repeat Search				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155361				0	en-ZA	With the \<emph\>Repeat search\</emph\> icon on the \<emph\>Navigation\</emph\> toolbar you can repeat a search you started with the \<emph\>Search and Replace\</emph\> dialogue. To do this, click the icon. The blue arrow buttons on the vertical scrollbar now take on the functions \<emph\>Continue search forwards\</emph\> and \<emph\>Continue search backwards\</emph\>. If you now click one of the arrow surfaces, the search will be continued for the term entered in the Search and Replace dialogue.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\swriter\01\02110100.xhp	0	help	par_idN106DD				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Select this icon to browse through bookmarks.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
