helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154395				0	en-ZA	... the field name contains a value that lies between the two values x and y.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154561				0	en-ZA	NOT BETWEEN x AND y				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148753				0	en-ZA	NOT BETWEEN x AND y				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155498				0	en-ZA	Does not fall within the interval [x,y]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148992				0	en-ZA	... the field name contains a value that does not lie between the two values x and y.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149995				0	en-ZA	IN (a; b; c...)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159167				0	en-ZA	Note that the semicolons are used as separators in all value lists!				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159085				0	en-ZA	IN (a, b, c...)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154809				0	en-ZA	contains a, b, c...				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148399				0	en-ZA	... the field name contains one of the specified expressions a, b, c,... Any number of expressions can be specified, and the result of the query is determined by an Or link. The expressions a, b, c... can be either numbers or characters				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154112				0	en-ZA	NOT IN (a; b; c...)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153544				0	en-ZA	NOT IN (a, b, c...)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150679				0	en-ZA	does not contain a, b, c...				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158439				0	en-ZA	... the field name does not contain one of the specified expressions a, b, c,...				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145145				0	en-ZA	= TRUE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146804				0	en-ZA	= TRUE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149248				0	en-ZA	has the value True				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148524				0	en-ZA	... the field name has the value True.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159212				0	en-ZA	= FALSE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144751				0	en-ZA	= FALSE				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149955				0	en-ZA	has the value false				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146850				0	en-ZA	... the field name has the value false.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155954				0	en-ZA	Examples				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153792				0	en-ZA	='Ms.'				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150948				0	en-ZA	returns field names with the field content "Ms."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150333				0	en-ZA	LIKE 'g?ve'				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147332				0	en-ZA	returns field names with field content such as "give" and "gave".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146062				0	en-ZA	LIKE 'S*'				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155350				0	en-ZA	returns data fields with field contents such as "Sun".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152883				0	en-ZA	BETWEEN 10 AND 20				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159406				0	en-ZA	returns field names with field content between the values 10 and 20. (The fields can be either text fields or number fields).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148765				0	en-ZA	IN (1; 3; 5; 7)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149712				0	en-ZA	returns field names with the values 1, 3, 5, 7. If the field name contains an item number, for example, you can create a query that returns the item having the specified number.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152948				0	en-ZA	NOT IN ('Smith')				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147279				0	en-ZA	returns field names that do not contain "Smith".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157998				0	en-ZA	\<emph\>Date fields\</emph\> are represented as #Date# to clearly identify them as dates. The date condition will be reproduced in the resulting SQL statement in the following ODBC - compliant way:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153734	253			0	en-ZA	Date				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159131	254			0	en-ZA	{D'YYYY-MM-DD'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153937	255			0	en-ZA	Date time				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146090	256			0	en-ZA	{D'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155947	257			0	en-ZA	Time				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150964	258			0	en-ZA	{D'HH:MM:SS'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151220	260			0	en-ZA	$[officename] also supports the following \<emph\>Escape sequences\</emph\> known from ODBC and JDBC:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157975	261			0	en-ZA	Date				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149753	262			0	en-ZA	{d 'YYYY-MM-DD'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156318	263			0	en-ZA	Time				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151280	264			0	en-ZA	{t 'HH:MI:SS[.SS]'} - [ ] optional				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153264	265			0	en-ZA	DateTime				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153981	266			0	en-ZA	{ts 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MI:SS[.SS]'} - [ ] optional				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149539				0	en-ZA	Example: select {d '1999-12-31'} from world.years				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146073	268			0	en-ZA	\<emph\>Like \</emph\>Escape Sequence: {escape 'escape-character'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150661				0	en-ZA	Example: select * from Item where ItemName like 'The *%' {escape '*'}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148541				0	en-ZA	The example will give you all of the entries where the item name begins with 'The *'. This means that you can also search for characters that would otherwise be interpreted as placeholders, such as *, ?, _, % or the period.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150572				0	en-ZA	\<emph\>Outer Join\</emph\> Escape Sequence: {oj outer-join}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156052				0	en-ZA	Example: select Article.* from {oj item LEFT OUTER JOIN orders ON item.no=orders.ANR}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153674				0	en-ZA	Querying text fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149134				0	en-ZA	To query the content of a text field, you must put the expression between single quotes. The distinction between uppercase and lowercase letters depends on the database in use. LIKE, by definition, is case-sensitive (though some databases don't see it that strict).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149302				0	en-ZA	Querying date fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150510				0	en-ZA	Even if you want to filter by a date, you must place the expression between single quotation marks. The following formats are valid: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS and YYYY/MM/DD HH:MM:SS as well as YYYY.MM.DD HH:MM:SS				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150427				0	en-ZA	Querying Yes/No fields				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149523				0	en-ZA	To query Yes/No fields, use the following syntax for dBASE tables:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153180				0	en-ZA	Status				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147481				0	en-ZA	Query criterion				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155187				0	en-ZA	Example				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156092				0	en-ZA	Yes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152414				0	en-ZA	for dBASE tables: not equal to any given value				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151265				0	en-ZA	=1 returns all records where the Yes/No field has the status "Yes" or "On" (selected in black),				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152450				0	en-ZA	No				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150997				0	en-ZA	.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155331				0	en-ZA	=0 returns all records for which the Yes/No field has the status "No" or "Off" (no selection).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154179				0	en-ZA	Null				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147035				0	en-ZA	IS NULL				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159385				0	en-ZA	IS NULL returns all records for which the Yes/No field has neither of the states Yes or No (selected in grey).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157888				0	en-ZA	The syntax depends on the database system used. You should also note that Yes/No fields can be defined differently (only 2 states instead of 3).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145772				0	en-ZA	Parameter queries				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157312				0	en-ZA	You must place the variable between square brackets (=[x]) to create a query with variable parameters. Alternatively, you can use an equal sign followed by a colon (=:x). When the query is executed, the program will display a dialogue asking you for the expression to which the variable x should be assigned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150818				0	en-ZA	If you query several parameters at the same time, you will see a list field in the dialogue containing all of the parameters and an input line alongside each one. Enter the values, preferably from top to bottom, and press the Enter key after each line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157855				0	en-ZA	Parameter queries with placeholders (*, _) or special characters (for example, ?) are not possible.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157537				0	en-ZA	If you formulate a parameter query and you save it with the variables, you can later create a query in which only the variables have to be replaced by the expressions that you want. $[officename] asks for these variables in a dialogue as soon as you open the query.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151035				0	en-ZA	Parameter Input				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153596				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>The \<emph\>Parameter Input\</emph\> dialogue asks you which variables you defined in the query. Enter a value for each query variable and confirm by clicking \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150585				0	en-ZA	Parameter queries are also used for \<link href=\"text/shared/02/01170203.xhp\" name=\"subforms\"\>subforms\</link\>, since they work exclusively with queries for which the values to be invoked are read internally from a variable.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153645				0	en-ZA	A parameter query can have the following form in an SQL statement:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156731				0	en-ZA	select * from 'addresses' where 'name' = :placeholder				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145181				0	en-ZA	SQL Mode				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147013				0	en-ZA	SQL stands for "Structured Query Language" and describes instructions for updating and administering relational databases.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152570	136			0	en-ZA	In $[officename] you do not need any knowledge of SQL for most queries, since you do not have to enter the SQL code. If you create a query in the query design, $[officename] automatically converts your instructions into the corresponding SQL syntax. If, with the help of the \<emph\>Switch Design View On/Off \</emph\>button, you change to the SQL view, you can see the SQL commands for a query that has been created previously.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152412				0	en-ZA	You can formulate your query directly in the SQL code. Note, however, that the special syntax is dependent upon the database system that you use.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146842				0	en-ZA	If you enter the SQL code manually, you can create SQL-specific queries that are not supported by the graphical interface in \<emph\>Query design\</emph\>. These queries must be executed in native SQL mode.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149632				0	en-ZA	By clicking the \<link href=\"text/shared/02/14030000.xhp\" name=\"Run SQL command directly\"\>\<emph\>Run SQL command directly\</emph\>\</link\> icon in the SQL view, you can formulate a query that is not processed by $[officename].				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-ZA	Properties 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	hd_id8836939				0	en-ZA	\<variable id=\"rep_prop\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/rep_prop.xhp\"\>Properties\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id2711264				0	en-ZA	The Properties window of the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/rep_main.xhp\"\>Report Builder\</link\> always shows the properties of the currently selected object in the Report Builder view.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id1080660				0	en-ZA	Press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Shift-F1\</item\> and point with the mouse at an input box to see a help text for this input box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id7175817				0	en-ZA	On first start of the Report Builder, the Properties window shows the \<emph\>Data\</emph\> tab page for the whole report.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id9895931				0	en-ZA	Select a table from the Contents list, then press Tab or click outside the input box to leave the input box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id3587145				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>The Add Field window is shown automatically when you have selected a table in the Contents box and leave that box. You can also click the Add Field icon on the toolbar, or choose \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>View - Add Field\</item\>.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id6993926				0	en-ZA	The \<emph\>General\</emph\> tab page can be used to change the name of the report, and to disable the Page Header or Page Footer areas, among others.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id3729361				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\"\>To display the Data or General tab page for the whole report, choose Edit - Select Report.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id1768852				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Groups are kept together by page or by column (default). You must enable Keep Together also.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id6304818				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Specifies in which context the page header will be printed: on all pages, or not on pages with a report header or footer.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id401623				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Specifies in which context the page footer will be printed: on all pages, or not on pages with a report header or footer\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id2162236				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Specifies to print repeated values.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id7022003				0	en-ZA	If you click the Page Header or Page Footer area without selecting any object, you see the \<emph\>General\</emph\> tab page for that area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id7004303				0	en-ZA	You can edit some visual properties for the area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id2561723				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Sets the background colour for the selected object, both on screen and for printing.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\rep_prop.xhp	0	help	par_id1064485				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>An invisible object is not shown in the executed report. It is still visible in the Report Builder view.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
