helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070200.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-ZA	Insert Name				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070200.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153195				0	en-ZA	\<bookmark_value\>cell ranges; inserting named ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting; cell ranges\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153195				0	en-ZA	Insert Name				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070200.xhp	0	help	par_id3150011				0	en-ZA	\<variable id=\"nameneinfuegentext\"\>\<ahelp hid=\".uno:InsertName\"\>Inserts a defined named cell range at the current cursor's position.\</ahelp\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070200.xhp	0	help	par_id3149412				0	en-ZA	You can only insert a cell area after having defined a name for the area.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153160				0	en-ZA	Insert name 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070200.xhp	0	help	par_id3154944				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:LISTBOX:RID_SCDLG_NAMES_PASTE:LB_ENTRYLIST\"\>Lists all defined cell areas. Double-click an entry to insert the named area into the active sheet at the current cursor position.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070200.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153418				0	en-ZA	Insert All				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04070200.xhp	0	help	par_id3155066				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:PUSHBUTTON:RID_SCDLG_NAMES_PASTE:BTN_ADD\"\>Inserts a list of all named areas and the corresponding cell references at the current cursor position.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-ZA	AutoInput				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	bm_id2486037				0	en-ZA	\<bookmark_value\>entering entries with AutoInput function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>capital letters;AutoInput function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148492				0	en-ZA	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/06130000.xhp\" name=\"AutoInput\"\>AutoInput\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150793				0	en-ZA	\<ahelp hid=\".uno:AutoComplete\"\>Switches the AutoInput function on and off, which automatically completes entries, based on other entries in the same column.\</ahelp\> The column is scanned up to a maximum of 2000 cells or 200 different strings.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_id3156422				0	en-ZA	The completion text is highlighted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_idN1065D				0	en-ZA	To accept the completion, press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Enter\</item\> or a cursor key.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_idN10665				0	en-ZA	To append text or to edit the completion, press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>F2\</item\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_idN1066D				0	en-ZA	To view more completions, press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Tab\</item\> to scroll forward, or \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Shift+Tab\</item\> to scroll backward.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_idN10679				0	en-ZA	To see a list of all available AutoInput text items for the current column, press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+D.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150439				0	en-ZA	When typing formulae using characters that match previous entries, a Help tip will appear listing the last ten functions used from \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>, from all defined range names, from all database range names, and from the content of all label ranges.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06130000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153363				0	en-ZA	AutoInput is case-sensitive. If, for example, you have written "Total" in a cell, you cannot enter "total" in another cell of the same column without first deactivating AutoInput.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	tit				0	en-ZA	Array Functions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147273				0	en-ZA	\<bookmark_value\>matrices; functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>Function Wizard; arrays\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>array formulae\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inline array constants\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formulae;arrays\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions;array functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>editing; array formulae\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>copying; array formulae\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>adjusting array ranges\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>calculating;conditional calculations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>matrices; calculations\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>conditional calculations with arrays\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>implicit array handling\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>forced array handling\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147273				0	en-ZA	Array Functions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154744				0	en-ZA	\<variable id=\"matrixtext\"\>This category contains the array functions. \</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146084				0	en-ZA	What is an Array?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154298				0	en-ZA	\<variable id=\"wasmatrix\"\>An array is a linked range of cells on a spreadsheet containing values. \</variable\> A square range of 3 rows and 3 columns is a 3 x 3 array:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154692				0	en-ZA	A				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150117				0	en-ZA	B				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155325				0	en-ZA	C				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3153104				0	en-ZA	1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3146996				0	en-ZA	\<item type=\"input\"\>7\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150529				0	en-ZA	\<item type=\"input\"\>31\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148831				0	en-ZA	\<item type=\"input\"\>33\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148943				0	en-ZA	2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149771				0	en-ZA	\<item type=\"input\"\>95\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158407				0	en-ZA	\<item type=\"input\"\>17\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148806				0	en-ZA	\<item type=\"input\"\>2\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154904				0	en-ZA	3				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150779				0	en-ZA	\<item type=\"input\"\>5\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148449				0	en-ZA	\<item type=\"input\"\>10\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3147238				0	en-ZA	\<item type=\"input\"\>50\</item\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3153583				0	en-ZA	The smallest possible array is a 1 x 2 or 2 x 1 array with two adjacent cells.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148474				0	en-ZA	What is an array formula?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3155355				0	en-ZA	A formula in which the individual values in a cell range are evaluated is referred to as an array formula. The difference between an array formula and other formulae is that the array formula deals with several values simultaneously instead of just one.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151052				0	en-ZA	Not only can an array formula process several values, but it can also return several values. The results of an array formula is also an array.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158432				0	en-ZA	To multiply the values in the individual cells by 10 in the above array, you do not need to apply a formula to each individual cell or value. Instead you just need to use a single array formula. Select a range of 3 x 3 cells on another part of the spreadsheet, enter the formula \<item type=\"input\"\>=10*A1:C3\</item\> and confirm this entry using the key combination \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+Enter. The result is a 3 x 3 array in which the individual values in the cell range (A1:C3) are multiplied by a factor of 10.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149156				0	en-ZA	In addition to multiplication, you can also use other operators on the reference range (an array). With $[officename] Calc, you can add (+), subtract (-), multiply (*), divide (/), use exponents (^), concatenation (&) and comparisons (=, <>, <, >, <=, >=). The operators can be used on each individual value in the cell range and return the result as an array if the array formula was entered.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3166456				0	en-ZA	Comparison operators in an array formula treat empty cells in the same way as in a normal formula, that is, either as zero or as an empty string. For example, if cells A1 and A2 are empty the array formulae \<item type=\"input\"\>{=A1:A2=""}\</item\> and \<item type=\"input\"\>{=A1:A2=0}\</item\> will both return a 1 column 2 row array of cells containing TRUE.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150713				0	en-ZA	When do you use array formulae?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149787				0	en-ZA	Use array formulae if you have to repeat calculations using different values. If you decide to change the calculation method later, you only have to update the array formula. To add an array formula, select the entire array range and then \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060107.xhp\" name=\"make the required change to the array formula\"\>make the required change to the array formula\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149798				0	en-ZA	Array formulae are also a space saving option when several values must be calculated, since they are not very memory-intensive. In addition, arrays are an essential tool for carrying out complex calculations, because you can have several cell ranges included in your calculations. $[officename] has different maths functions for arrays, such as the MMULT function for multiplying two arrays or the SUMPRODUCT function for calculating the scalar products of two arrays.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155588				0	en-ZA	Using Array Formulae in $[officename] Calc				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3152876				0	en-ZA	You can also create a "normal" formula in which the reference range, such as parameters, indicate an array formula. The result is obtained from the intersection of the reference range and the rows or columns in which the formula is found. If there is no intersection or if the range at the intersection covers several rows or columns, a #VALUE! error message appears. The following example illustrates this concept:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151271				0	en-ZA	Creating Array Formulae				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149102				0	en-ZA	If you create an array formula using the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>, you must mark the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box each time so that the results are returned in an array. Otherwise, only the value in the upper-left cell of the array being calculated is returned.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3153392				0	en-ZA	If you enter the array formula directly into the cell, you must use the key combination Shift+\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Enter instead of the Enter key. Only then does the formula become an array formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151120				0	en-ZA	Array formulae appear in braces in $[officename] Calc. You cannot create array formulae by manually entering the braces.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154342				0	en-ZA	The cells in a results array are automatically protected against changes. However, you can edit or copy the array formula by selecting the entire array cell range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id8834803				0	en-ZA	Using Inline Array Constants in Formulas				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id985747				0	en-ZA	Calc supports inline matrix/array constants in formulae. An inline array is surrounded by curly braces '{' and '}'. Elements can be each a number (including negatives), a logical constant (TRUE, FALSE), or a literal string. Non-constant expressions are not allowed. Arrays can be entered with one or more rows, and one or more columns. All rows must consist of the same number of elements, all columns must consist of the same number of elements. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id936613				0	en-ZA	The column separator (separating elements in one row) is the ';' semicolon. The row separator is a '|' pipe symbol. The separators are not language and locale dependent. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id1877498				0	en-ZA	Arrays can not be nested.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id4262520				0	en-ZA	\<emph\>Examples:\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id9387493				0	en-ZA	={1;2;3}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id8207037				0	en-ZA	An array with one row consisting of the three numbers 1, 2, and 3.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id6757103				0	en-ZA	To enter this array constant, you select three cells in a row, then you type the formula \<item type=\"input\"\>={1;2;3}\</item\> using the curly braces and the semicolons, then press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+Enter.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id8868068				0	en-ZA	={1;2;3|4;5;6}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id6626483				0	en-ZA	An array with two rows and three values in each row.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id5262916				0	en-ZA	={0;1;2|FALSE;TRUE;"two"}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id1623889				0	en-ZA	A mixed data array.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id7781914				0	en-ZA	=SIN({1;2;3})				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id300912				0	en-ZA	Entered as a matrix formula, delivers the result of three SIN calculations with the arguments 1, 2, and 3.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148660				0	en-ZA	Editing Array Formulae				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149241				0	en-ZA	Select the cell range or array containing the array formula. To select the whole array, position the cell cursor inside the array range, then press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+ /, where / is the Division key on the numeric keypad.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3143274				0	en-ZA	Either press F2 or position the cursor in the input line. Both of these actions let you edit the formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154798				0	en-ZA	After you have made changes, press \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+Enter.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150628				0	en-ZA	You can format the separate parts of an array. For example, you can change the font colour. Select a cell range and then change the attribute you want.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145608				0	en-ZA	Copying Array Formulae				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3149585				0	en-ZA	Select the cell range or array containing the array formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154619				0	en-ZA	Either press F2 or position the cursor in the input line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150994				0	en-ZA	Copy the formula into the input line by pressing \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+C.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3146787				0	en-ZA	Select a range of cells where you want to insert the array formula and either press F2 or position the cursor in the input line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3154419				0	en-ZA	Paste the formula by pressing \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+V in the selected space and confirm it by pressing \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>+Shift+Enter. The selected range now contains the array formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154834				0	en-ZA	Adjusting an Array Range				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3148679				0	en-ZA	If you want to edit the output array, do the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3151102				0	en-ZA	Select the cell range or array containing the array formula.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3147096				0	en-ZA	Below the selection, to the right, you will see a small icon with which you can zoom in or out on the range using your mouse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3150974				0	en-ZA	When you adjust the array range, the array formula will not automatically be adjusted. You are only changing the range in which the result will appear.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3146080				0	en-ZA	By holding down the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>Command \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Ctrl \</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>key, you can create a copy of the array formula in the given range.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D47				0	en-ZA	Conditional Array Calculations				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D4B				0	en-ZA	A conditional array calculation is an array or matrix formula that includes an IF() or CHOOSE() function. The condition argument in the formula is an area reference or a matrix result.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D4E				0	en-ZA	In the following example, the >0 test of the {=IF(A1:A3>0;"yes";"no")} formula is applied to each cell in the range A1:A3 and the result is copied to the corresponding cell.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D65				0	en-ZA	A				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D6B				0	en-ZA	B (formula)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10B75				0	en-ZA	B (result)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D79				0	en-ZA	1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D80				0	en-ZA	1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D86				0	en-ZA	{=IF(A1:A3>0;"yes";"no")}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D8C				0	en-ZA	yes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D94				0	en-ZA	2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10D9B				0	en-ZA	0				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DA1				0	en-ZA	{=IF(A1:A3>0;"yes";"no")}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DA7				0	en-ZA	no				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DAF				0	en-ZA	3				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DB6				0	en-ZA	1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DBC				0	en-ZA	{=IF(A1:A3>0;"yes";"no")}				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DC2				0	en-ZA	yes				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DD0				0	en-ZA	The following functions provide forced array handling: CORREL, COVAR, FORECAST, FTEST, INTERCEPT, MDETERM, MINVERSE, MMULT, MODE, PEARSON, PROB, RSQ, SLOPE, STEYX, SUMPRODUCT, SUMX2MY2, SUMX2PY2, SUMXMY2, TTEST. If you use area references as arguments when you call one of these functions, the functions behave as array functions. The following table provides an example of forced array handling:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DE2				0	en-ZA	A				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DE8				0	en-ZA	B (formula)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DEE				0	en-ZA	B (result)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DF4				0	en-ZA	C (forced array formula)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10DFA				0	en-ZA	C (result)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E02				0	en-ZA	1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E09				0	en-ZA	1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E0F				0	en-ZA	=A1:A2+1				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E17				0	en-ZA	2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E1D				0	en-ZA	=SUMPRODUCT(A1:A2+1)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E25				0	en-ZA	5				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E2D				0	en-ZA	2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN10E34				0	en-ZA	2				2002-02-02 02:02:02
