helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03100700.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146958				0	en-ZA	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03100700.xhp\" name=\"Const Statement [Runtime]\"\>Const Statement [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03100700.xhp	0	help	par_id3154143				0	en-ZA	Defines a string as a constant.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03100700.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150670				0	en-ZA	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03100700.xhp	0	help	par_id3150984				0	en-ZA	Const Text = Expression				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03100700.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147530				0	en-ZA	Parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03100700.xhp	0	help	par_id3153897				0	en-ZA	\<emph\>Text:\</emph\> Any constant name that follows the standard variable naming conventions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03100700.xhp	0	help	par_id3147264				0	en-ZA	A constant is a variable that helps to improve the readability of a program. Constants are not defined as a specific type of variable, but rather are used as placeholders in the code. You can only define a constant once and it cannot be modified. Use the following statement to define a constant:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03100700.xhp	0	help	par_id3150542				0	en-ZA	CONST ConstName=Expression				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03100700.xhp	0	help	par_id3150400				0	en-ZA	The type of expression is irrelevant. If a program is started, $[officename] Basic converts the program code internally so that each time a constant is used, the defined expression replaces it.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03100700.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154366				0	en-ZA	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
