helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154140				0	en-ZA	\<variable id=\"show_dialog\"\>\<link href=\"text/sbasic/guide/show_dialog.xhp\" name=\"Opening a Dialogue With Program Code\"\>Opening a Dialogue With Program Code\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171				0	en-ZA	In the \<item type=\"productname\"\>%PRODUCTNAME\</item\> BASIC window for a dialogue that you created, leave the dialogue editor by clicking the name tab of the Module that the dialogue is assigned to. The name tab is at the bottom of the window.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3153968				0	en-ZA	Enter the following code for a subroutine called \<emph\>Dialog1Show\</emph\>. In this example, the name of the dialogue that you created is "Dialog1":				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3156443				0	en-ZA	Sub Dialog1Show				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3148575				0	en-ZA	BasicLibraries.LoadLibrary("Tools")				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3152463				0	en-ZA	oDialog1 = \<link href=\"text/sbasic/guide/sample_code.xhp\" name=\"LoadDialog\"\>LoadDialog\</link\>("Standard", "Dialog1")				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3148646				0	en-ZA	oDialog1.Execute()				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3147349				0	en-ZA	End Sub				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\guide\show_dialog.xhp	0	help	par_id3152596				0	en-ZA	Without using "LoadDialog" you can call the code as follows:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
